// expect: error
// expect-error: E004

// GAP — owner: KI-086, High, 0.0.8.
// Measured 2026-09-11: error[E004] Undefined Variable — I can't find a value
// named `name`, at the `name(x)` in the default body below.
//
// A class default body cannot call a sibling method of its own class when the
// class is declared inside a `module`. The identical class at the top level of
// a script compiles and runs.
//
// This is one of KI-086's *two* failures, and the only one this corpus can
// pin. The other is a run-time one: across a module boundary the default body
// is silently dropped and dispatch panics with
// `error[E1009]: panic: No instance of Greet.greet for the given type`.
//
// That second failure is deliberately not filed as a ../runtime/ file, because
// no harness here can see it. It needs the `.flxi` interface round-trip — the
// entry's cause is that a class rebuilt from a cached interface entry gets
// `default_body: None`, so method *types* cross the boundary and method
// *bodies* do not. The runtime harness passes `--no-cache`, and measured on
// 2026-09-11 that flag makes the program print "hi, someone" and exit 0. A
// snapshot taken there would pin the bug *not* reproducing, which is worse
// than no snapshot: whoever fixes KI-086 would see no diff.
//
//   flux --no-strict            ki_086_repro.flx   ->  E1009 panic
//   flux --no-cache --no-strict ki_086_repro.flx   ->  prints "hi, someone"
//
// That `--no-cache` hides it is a usable workaround and a sharper statement of
// the cause than the entry carries; KI-086 should record it.
//
// A single file is enough here because the error is raised inside the module
// itself, so no importing script is needed to provoke it.

module Ki086Greet {
    public class Greet<a> {
        fn name(x: a) -> String
        fn greet(x: a) -> String { "hi, " + name(x) }
    }
}
